{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Of every clean beast you shall take for yourself seven pairs.</b> It would have sufficed to take two pairs for the survival of the species, but He commanded him to take seven of them for the purpose of sacrifice. And behold, this number is compelled by the mystery of the sacrifices. You already know from the wisdom of Balaam — for our Sages of blessed memory said (Deuteronomy 34:10): ״ולא קם נביא עוד בישראל כמשה״ — 'in Israel' he did not arise, but among the nations of the world he did arise, and they counted Balaam — that he commanded Balak and said (Numbers 23:1): ״בנה לי בזה שבעה מזבחות והכן לי בזה שבעה פרים ושבעה אילים״, and he further said (Numbers ibid.): ״את שבעת המזבחות ערכתי״." "<b>And behold,</b> YHWH, blessed be He, the Father of mercies, did not wish to decree that they should come of themselves for the purpose of sacrifice, to be slaughtered, but said: ״תקח לך שבעה״ — that he should exert himself after them and toil in the commandment. But those that were for keeping seed alive were two pairs, and it was decreed that they should come of themselves; that is what is written: ״יבאו אליך להחיות״. And it also says ״גם מעוף השמים״, and the verse continues from above: it says 'also of the fowl of the heavens,' the clean ones, seven pairs as mentioned, ״איש ואשתו״ — that is, seven males and seven females — and He added to them yet one male and one female to keep seed alive upon the face of all the earth. And what compelled us to interpret so is that for the survival of the species, why would seven be needed — two would suffice. Or we may say: ״להחיות זרע על פני כל הארץ״ refers back to what He mentioned — two, male and female — and it is as though the verse says: 'Of every clean beast you shall take for yourself seven pairs, male and female; also of the fowl of the heavens seven pairs, male and female' — and all this is for sacrifice — 'and of the beast that is not clean, they are two, male and female, to keep seed alive upon the face of all the earth'; ״כי לימים עוד שבעה עתיד אני להביא מבול״ — and therefore you will need to take and sustain the species yourself." "<b>And the reason</b> that He mentioned for the species of beast ״איש ואשתו״ and for the species of fowl 'male and female' is that the nature of reproduction is not the same in them, for the offspring of beasts are like the offspring of the human species, and therefore He mentioned ״איש ואשתו״; but the offspring of the fowl species come about through the laying of eggs, which are themselves the beginning of their formation." "<b>And the opinion</b> of our Sages of blessed memory in chapter ״חלק״ is that because the beasts did not mate with those not of their kind, He showed them honor and wrote of them the term אישות — 'man and wife' — as with humans. And you need to know that in the section above, in which the Holy One, blessed be He, informed Noah that He would bring the Flood, He began with the attribute of justice — ״ויאמר אלהים לנח קץ כל בשר״ — and concluded as well with the attribute of justice: ״ככל אשר צוה אותו אלהים כן עשה״. But in this section, in which He hinted to him concerning the sacrifice, He begins with the attribute of mercy — ״ויאמר ה' לנח״ — and concludes with the attribute of mercy: ״ויעש נח ככל אשר צוהו ה'״. And all this is to indicate that the intent of the sacrifice is solely for the Name YHWH, blessed be He. That is what our Sages of blessed memory expounded: all the sacrifices in the Torah never mention 'El' nor 'Elohim' nor 'Eloah' but only the particular Name — so as not to give an opening to the adversary to dispute."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The citation (Deuteronomy 34:10) introduces the well-known rabbinic gloss on the phrase; R. Bahya attributes it to the Sages rather than reading it as the plain verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:11:08.367016344Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}